                <h2>Background</h2>
<p>Overseas advertising involves multiple platforms such as Meta, Google, TikTok, and Pinterest, with multiple recharge channels, long reconciliation cycles, and complex currency exchange rates. Many teams neglect reconciliation standardization while chasing spend targets, leading to difficulties in month-end financial verification and inaccurate balance data. This SOP covers the unified reconciliation process for three major data sources: recharge records, platform spend, and invoice verification.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Recharge Record Verification</h2>
<h3>1.1 Collect Recharge Vouchers</h3>
<p>Within 24 hours of completing a recharge, operations should sync the following information to finance:<br />
- Recharge platform (Meta Business Center / Google Ads / TikTok Ads)<br />
- Recharge time (UTC)<br />
- Recharge amount and currency (USD, EUR, GBP, etc.)<br />
- Recharge method (credit card, PayPal, bank transfer, third-party like PingPong/Wise)<br />
- Transaction ID or recharge confirmation screenshot</p>
<h3>1.2 Exchange Rate Conversion</h3>
<p>Use the bank's middle rate or platform settlement rate on the recharge day for conversion and record the conversion formula. Note that third-party payment platforms may charge additional fees, which should be listed separately. It is recommended to maintain an exchange rate record sheet and update it promptly after each recharge.</p>
<h3>1.3 Mark "Credited" Status</h3>
<p>Recharge credits may be delayed by 1-48 hours. Operations should regularly check account balance changes, confirm credit arrival, and mark the status to avoid duplicate or missed recharges.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Platform Spend Data Extraction</h2>
<h3>2.1 Determine Reconciliation Cycle</h3>
<p>It is recommended to extract spend data on a weekly or monthly basis. Data update frequencies vary by platform:<br />
- <strong>Meta</strong>: Spend data is typically delayed by 2-4 hours; month-end bills are generated on the 3rd-5th of the following month<br />
- <strong>Google Ads</strong>: Real-time spend updates; month-end bills are confirmed around the 5th of the following month<br />
- <strong>TikTok Ads</strong>: Spend data has a 1-hour delay; billing cycle is similar to Meta</p>
<h3>2.2 Data Extraction Methods</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Manual export</strong>: Export CSV from Ads Manager or Google Ads interface; ensure correct timezone and date range</li>
<li><strong>API extraction</strong>: Teams with technical capabilities can call the official Reporting API for automated data synchronization</li>
<li><strong>Third-party tools</strong>: One cross-border e-commerce team uses Feishu (Lark) multi-dimensional tables connected to DataTower.ai for automatic spend data aggregation</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.3 Standardize Data Fields</h3>
<p>When extracting data, consistently retain: Account ID, Date, Spend Amount (original currency), Spend Amount (USD equivalent), Spend Type (bidding spend, taxes, etc.)</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Invoice Acquisition and Verification</h2>
<h3>3.1 Identify Invoice Types</h3>
<p>Overseas advertising platform invoices are divided into two categories:<br />
- <strong>Proforma Invoice</strong>: Generated at recharge, used for prepayment confirmation; not a tax certificate<br />
- <strong>Tax Invoice / VAT Invoice</strong>: Generated after month-end settlement; can be used for export tax refunds or cost accounting</p>
<p>Operations must confirm which invoice type finance needs to avoid premature collection or delayed receipt.</p>
<h3>3.2 Invoice Acquisition Paths</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Platform</th>
<th>Invoice Location</th>
<th>Delivery Time</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta</td>
<td>Business Settings → Payments → Invoices</td>
<td>5th-10th of following month</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Ads</td>
<td>Tools → Billing → Documents</td>
<td>3rd-7th of following month</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok Ads</td>
<td>Assets → Billing Center → Invoices</td>
<td>5th-12th of following month</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>3.3 Invoice Verification Points</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check if invoice amount matches spend data (note: platform may deduct refunds or adjustments)</li>
<li>Verify tax ID, company name, address, and other billing information accuracy</li>
<li>Confirm invoice currency matches account spend currency</li>
<li>Save PDF originals, archived as "Platform+YearMonth"</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Three-Way Matching and Difference Handling</h2>
<h3>4.1 Cross-Reference Three Data Sources</h3>
<p>After completing the first three steps, cross-compare:<br />
1. <strong>Recharge amount</strong>: Third-party payment platform debit record<br />
2. <strong>Spend amount</strong>: Bill spend pulled from platform backend<br />
3. <strong>Invoice amount</strong>: Formal invoice issued by platform</p>
<p>Ideally, all three should be consistent, but common differences in actual operations include:</p>
<h3>4.2 Common Difference Types and Handling Methods</h3>
<p><strong>Difference Type 1: Recharge Not Fully Spent</strong><br />
- Cause: Month-end recharge not yet fully spent, or account has remaining balance<br />
- Handling: Keep balance record, count as "prepaid expense," continue deduction next period</p>
<p><strong>Difference Type 2: Invoice Amount Lower Than Spend</strong><br />
- Cause: Platform prepaid tax, adjustment items, or refunds not reflected in invoice<br />
- Handling: Request platform customer service for adjustment details; finance records adjusted amount</p>
<p><strong>Difference Type 3: Amount Deviation Due to Exchange Rate</strong><br />
- Cause: Recharge rate, platform settlement rate, and invoice rate are inconsistent<br />
- Handling: Set tolerance threshold (e.g., ±0.5%); within range, record as financial expense</p>
<p><strong>Difference Type 4: Recharge Credit Delay</strong><br />
- Cause: Cross-bank transfer or third-party payment delay<br />
- Handling: Mark "recharge in transit," write off in next reconciliation period</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Ledger Setup and Periodic Review</h2>
<h3>5.1 Establish Reconciliation Ledger</h3>
<p>It is recommended to create a ledger template using Excel or Feishu with fields: Date, Platform, Recharge Amount, Spend Amount, Invoice Amount, Difference Amount, Difference Reason, Handling Status, Person in Charge.</p>
<h3>5.2 Monthly Review Checkpoints</h3>
<p>After each monthly reconciliation, operations and finance jointly confirm:<br />
- All differences have been resolved<br />
- No long-term pending prepaid items<br />
- Invoices are archived timely; tax declarations are synchronized<br />
- Recharge exchange rates need optimization (e.g., switch to more stable payment channels)</p>
<hr />
<h2>Execution Checklist</h2>
<p>Operations can quickly review the following checklist before each reconciliation:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Recharge vouchers are complete and synced to finance</li>
<li>[ ] Exchange rate conversion is documented</li>
<li>[ ] Account balance credit is confirmed</li>
<li>[ ] Spend data timezone and date range are correct</li>
<li>[ ] Invoices are downloaded and amounts verified</li>
<li>[ ] Three-way differences are annotated with reasons</li>
<li>[ ] Ledger is updated</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>For more information about Yiyu Media's overseas advertising account management services or financial reconciliation tool support, please contact us.</p>
